    "speed": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "h_speed": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why Freezing Speed Matters",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "t_speed": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Seafood muscle contains a great deal of water. During freezing, that water forms ice crystals &mdash; and the speed at which those crystals form can make a meaningful difference.</p><p>Slow freezing generally allows larger crystals to develop. These crystals can disrupt the fish&rsquo;s delicate cellular structure, leading to greater moisture loss, or &ldquo;purge,&rdquo; as the seafood thaws.</p><p>Rapid freezing moves the fish through this critical temperature range much faster. The result is typically smaller ice crystals, less structural damage, and better retention of the seafood&rsquo;s natural moisture and texture.</p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "h_speed",
        "t_speed"
      ],
