        "m_head": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Smaller crystals, better texture",
            "heading_size": "title-md",
            "heading_tag": "h2",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "m_text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Research on Atlantic salmon and other foods supports the relationship between freezing conditions, ice-crystal size, drip loss, and texture. The exact result varies by species, equipment, handling, storage, and thawing method.</p><ul><li>Smaller ice crystals help protect the fish&rsquo;s cellular structure.</li><li>Less structural damage can mean less moisture lost during thawing.</li><li>Better moisture retention supports a firmer, more succulent texture.</li><li>Freezing close to harvest preserves quality before time can take its toll.</li></ul><p><strong>Not all frozen seafood is created equal. The difference is how &mdash; and when &mdash; it was frozen.</strong></p>",
            "text_size": "subtext-md",
            "text_max_size": 32,
            "text_font": "body",
            "secondary_color": false,
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
